    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "83fce170_f494fd53",
        "filename": "generator/templates/api_cpp.h",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 342,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000032
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-07-17T18:54:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "well, wgpu::String allows both nil and non-nil strings which is more permissive than only allowing std::string_view. So the goal here was to make the C++ API a bit more typesafe.\n\nAlternatively, we could have two different string types WGPUString and WGPUOptionalString..? but they would be exactly the same struct in C. The only difference is how you can make it in C++. Also possible to only have that differentiation in the C++ header. So C just has WGPUString. C++ has wgpu::String and wgpu::OptionalString (or, NullableString)",
      "parentUuid": "e9e950ea_7e99fd4e",
      "revId": "4b8503ab527276bee9e41a85103285bcd861db4d",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
